<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seiyun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Say'un</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seiyun is a town in the Wadi Hadramawt in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. It was the capital of the Kathiri sultans, whose large white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> palace still stands in the town and now houses a museum. It lies between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a> and Tarim on the main route through the valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seiyun.jpg" alt="Seiyun" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JialiangGao www.peace-on-earth.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Al_Kathiri_Palace_Seiyun_Yemen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Seiyun lies in the middle of the Hadhramaut Valley, 12 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a> and 35 km from Tarim, on a flat plain of the Wadi Hadramaut between mountain ranges that lead up to the northern and southern plateaus. The district covers about 804 square kilometres. Summers are hot and winters mild, and rain is rare. Villages around the town include Madurah, Burr, Hazkir and Houta Sultana. Tradition says the place began as a resting point for travellers, named after a woman called Seiyun who kept a café there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Seiyun is first mentioned at the beginning of the 4th century, under King Dhamar, who ruled the Hadhramaut Valley. In the Islamic period it emerged as a village in the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> and was administratively part of Tarim, and from 708 part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a>. It became the capital of the valley under Sultan Badr Abu Tuwairq (1501-1556), who built a wall around it, ruled a sultanate that reached from Ain Ba to west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a> and made the Kathiri palace his residence, adding a mosque.</p><p>In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yafa">Yafa</a> took over much of Seiyun. In 1852 it became the capital of the Kathiri State, which lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">British Aden Protectorate</a>, and the sultanate's postage stamps were sometimes inscribed Kathiri State of Seiyun. Ghalib bin Muhsin Al Kathiri made the town his capital and the palace his seat, and his son Mansour bin Ghalib enlarged it with a new entrance and a large hall. After independence Seiyun became the capital of the southern district of the fifth province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Seiyun is a city of Hadhramaut Governorate, served by Seiyun Airport. Part of the mud-brick Kathiri Palace, also called the Dawil Palace, has been a museum since 1983, with finds from sites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hadhramaut">Wadi Hadhramaut</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">Ribat</a>, and documents of the Kathiri Sultanate. The town's Friday mosques include the Al-Jami Mosque, the oldest, and the Taha, Al-Qarn and Basalam mosques, and the hill fortresses of Qarat Al-Ar and Hisn Al-Fals stand within it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seiyun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kathiri sultans ruled from <b>Seiyun</b>, whose mud-brick palace still dominates the town, and from Tarim, the religious centre of the valley's sayyid families and Shafi'i scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/state/kathiri-sultanate">Kathiri Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
